course: dictionary
lesson: 8794
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one is meant. Share is a good example. «Chloe stays polite but stops sharing secrets.» Here sharing means telling others, because secrets are told, not cut up.
explain: «Share the pizza» means give each person some. Think of friends sharing a pizza: each one gets a slice. Swap your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Share can also be a noun, meaning one part that someone gets. «My share of the cake» is the piece that belongs to me. The same word can be a verb or a noun, so look at how it is used.
fact: Share is a verb and a noun: you can share something, or you can have a share of it.
fact: To share can mean to give parts of something to others, or to use something together, as in «share a room».
fact: To share can mean to tell others, as in «share your ideas» with a partner in class.
fact: Never share your password or your address online. Wait for trusted news before you share it.
fact: If 12 sweets are shared equally among 4 friends, each friend gets a share of 3 sweets.
exercise: «Chloe stays polite but stops sharing secrets.» In this sentence, sharing means | *Telling others | Hiding something | Buying a present | Cutting something into pieces
exercise: «Share the pizza» means | Wrap it up | Throw it away | *Give each person some of it | Put it in a box
exercise: Find and copy the word in «my share of the cake» that means one part that someone gets. | of | the | *share | cake
test: «Sharing silly jokes, like knock-knock jokes, builds friendship.» Here sharing means | Forgetting them quickly | Hiding them in a box | *Telling them to others so they can enjoy them | Writing them in a test
test: Mum says, «Share 12 sweets equally among 4 friends.» How many sweets does each friend get? | *3 | 48 | 2 | 8
exam: «Messages can be saved, shared and misunderstood, so you never send what you would not say face to face.» What does shared mean here? | Put in a lunchbox | *Passed on to others | Read aloud to a dog | Hidden in a drawer
